WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.210 --> 00:00:01.860
- [Narrator] Tell me if
this voice sounds familiar.

00:00:01.860 --> 00:00:04.610
- [Man] Sweet lion of Zion!

00:00:04.610 --> 00:00:05.550
- [Narrator] What about this one?

00:00:05.550 --> 00:00:08.770
- [Man] In brightest
day, in blackest night.

00:00:08.770 --> 00:00:10.890
- [Narrator] Okay, you
definitely know this one.

00:00:10.890 --> 00:00:14.810
- 50 years have passed, and I do not age.

00:00:14.810 --> 00:00:16.180
- [Narrator] We could
easily keep going through

00:00:16.180 --> 00:00:19.170
his 300 other characters, but
let's just cut to the chase

00:00:19.170 --> 00:00:20.883
and meet the man behind the voice.

00:00:23.332 --> 00:00:24.970
(energetic music)

00:00:24.970 --> 00:00:27.720
This is Phil LaMarr,
and he's a voice actor.

00:00:27.720 --> 00:00:30.570
- I'd say the ones that I'm
probably best known for,

00:00:30.570 --> 00:00:32.930
Hermes Conrad, certified bureaucrat,

00:00:32.930 --> 00:00:34.193
from the show “Futurama.”

00:00:35.881 --> 00:00:38.300
- Great cow of Moscow!

00:00:38.300 --> 00:00:41.440
Virgil, Virgil Hawkins, aka Static Shock,

00:00:41.440 --> 00:00:42.845
from “Static Shock.”

00:00:42.845 --> 00:00:46.270
John Stewart, Green Lantern,
from “Justice League.”

00:00:46.270 --> 00:00:48.720
- No evil shall escape my sight.

00:00:48.720 --> 00:00:51.120
- Ollie Williams, the
weatherman, from “Family Guy.”

00:00:51.120 --> 00:00:52.990
- Ollie Williams has the story, Ollie?

00:00:52.990 --> 00:00:54.400
- I'm at the wrong airport.

00:00:54.400 --> 00:00:55.550
- Oops, well thanks, Ollie.

00:00:55.550 --> 00:00:58.180
- Wilt, from “Foster's Home
for Imaginary Friends.”

00:00:58.180 --> 00:00:59.830
One of my favorites is Dracula

00:00:59.830 --> 00:01:01.970
from “Grim Adventures of Billy and Mandy.”

00:01:01.970 --> 00:01:06.483
And of course, the samurai
known as Jack, from the cities

00:01:06.483 --> 00:01:08.050
of the same name.

00:01:08.050 --> 00:01:12.040
- He who wears a mask cannot
see what lies within himself.

00:01:12.040 --> 00:01:14.209
- When I was working on “Pulp Fiction,”

00:01:14.209 --> 00:01:15.650
(record scratches)
- [Narrator] Wait, you were in

00:01:15.650 --> 00:01:16.780
“Pulp Fiction”?

00:01:16.780 --> 00:01:18.260
- Of course, yep.

00:01:18.260 --> 00:01:19.543
Here, let me jog your memory.

00:01:19.543 --> 00:01:21.136
(fun surf music)

00:01:21.136 --> 00:01:23.800
- Marvin, Marvin, Marvin!

00:01:23.800 --> 00:01:26.028
I'd knock that (expletive) off if I was you.

00:01:26.028 --> 00:01:28.148
- [Narrator] Ohhh, you're Marvin.

00:01:28.148 --> 00:01:30.930
- [Phil] And I was one of
the original cast members

00:01:30.930 --> 00:01:32.510
on Fox's “MADtv.”

00:01:32.510 --> 00:01:33.900
- [Narrator] No way, I love MADtv.

00:01:33.900 --> 00:01:36.910
- Then you must have been a
14-year-old boy in the ‘90s.

00:01:36.910 --> 00:01:39.170
- [Narrator] Very funny,
Phil, anyway, you were saying?

00:01:39.170 --> 00:01:43.735
- With Samurai Jack I felt
there should be some tie

00:01:43.735 --> 00:01:46.140
to his roots.

00:01:46.140 --> 00:01:51.140
So it was a very light Japanese
accent on English, we began

00:01:51.610 --> 00:01:55.920
to refer to as a young
Asian Clint Eastwood voice.

00:01:55.920 --> 00:01:57.430
- [Narrator] And Hermes from “Futurama,”

00:01:57.430 --> 00:01:58.844
where did that voice come from?

00:01:58.844 --> 00:02:00.950
- When I first started out
doing the Jamaican accent

00:02:00.950 --> 00:02:03.930
for Hermes, it was pretty
much a heavy puhtwah.

00:02:03.930 --> 00:02:07.022
And it muddied up the comedy,

00:02:07.022 --> 00:02:12.022
so eventually your Hermes
accent got pulled back,

00:02:12.400 --> 00:02:14.610
which is a lot easier to understand.

00:02:14.610 --> 00:02:16.510
- [Narrator] What about John
Stewart from “Justice League”?

00:02:16.510 --> 00:02:18.530
- To me, if you got a chest this big,

00:02:18.530 --> 00:02:20.097
you gotta have a deep voice.

00:02:20.097 --> 00:02:23.523
My name is John Stewart,
Green Lantern of Sector 2814.

00:02:23.523 --> 00:02:25.260
- [Narrator] And how about Static Shock?

00:02:25.260 --> 00:02:29.900
- That voice was basically
just myself at 14.

00:02:29.900 --> 00:02:32.782
It was just that idea
of, you know, a young kid,

00:02:32.782 --> 00:02:36.500
you know, who's a responsible guy but,
you know, he just happens

00:02:36.500 --> 00:02:37.710
to have electrical powers.

00:02:37.710 --> 00:02:40.168
And to me, that is the
character I relate to the most.

00:02:40.168 --> 00:02:44.163
You know, Static is just, you know, me.

00:02:44.163 --> 00:02:47.190
(lively jazz music)

00:02:47.190 --> 00:02:51.423
The differences between
stage, film, voice acting

00:02:51.423 --> 00:02:53.420
are really technical.

00:02:53.420 --> 00:02:55.408
If that plane leaves and you're not on it,

00:02:55.408 --> 00:02:56.241
you're gonna regret it.

00:02:56.241 --> 00:02:58.631
One of the benefits we have in animation

00:02:58.631 --> 00:03:01.231
is, I can play this guy, and
I can also play this guy.

00:03:03.380 --> 00:03:04.460
- You'll see what I'm talking about.

00:03:04.460 --> 00:03:06.460
- Just right there in
the same day, same room,

00:03:06.460 --> 00:03:09.420
same microphone, you know ‘cause
that's why we got into it,

00:03:09.420 --> 00:03:10.650
to play characters.

00:03:10.650 --> 00:03:13.867
You will bow before the Kah.

00:03:13.867 --> 00:03:16.867
(lively jazz music)

00:03:18.146 --> 00:03:21.729
(electronic tones playing)

